#
# How each suite verifies its subject, enforced by
# scripts/check_suite_execution.py and explained in CONTRIBUTING.md.
#
#   <class> <suite directory name>
#
# Classes
#
#   executes    Builds a program and runs it, asserting an exit value or
#               output. Detected mechanically: the suite invokes a produced
#               artifact in command position.
#
#   contract    Deliberately inspects text, JSON, or a diagnostic rather than
#               running a program, because that IS its subject: emitted IR
#               shape, a diagnostic message, a published document, a script
#               contract. Classifying a suite here asserts that no runtime
#               behavior of its feature is left unverified.
#
#   unreviewed  Not yet classified. The backlog is empty and the ceiling is
#               zero, so this class now exists only to describe the process:
#               a suite reaches `executes` or `contract` by being read, never
#               by a pattern match. Five of the seven defects fixed on
#               2026-09-04/05 hid behind suites that only asserted substrings
#               of emitted WIR -- test/loop-control matched every substring it
#               looked for while no `for` program could be built at all.
#
# Every suite is classified and the ceiling is 0, so a new suite must be
# classified in the same commit that adds it. Decide by reading it: if the
# feature has runtime behaviour nothing executes, add an execution case and
# mark it `executes`; if emitted text, a diagnostic, or a published document
# is genuinely the subject, mark it `contract` and say why on the line above.
#
# A `contract` rationale should name where the feature's runtime behaviour is
# executed, when it is executed somewhere else. That keeps the claim checkable
# instead of trusting the label.

